  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000119772,
      "SummaryText": "BRASKEM AMERICA, Inc. LA PORTE PLANT RMP EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\nACCIDENTAL RELEASE PREVENTION AND EMERGENCY RESPONSE POLICIES:\nAt the La Porte Plant, we are committed to operating and maintaining all of our processes in a safe and responsible manner. We use a combination of accidental release prevention programs and emergency response planning programs to help ensure the safety of our employees and the public as well as protection of the environment. This document provides a brief overview of the comprehensive risk management activities that we have designed and implemented, including:\n\n*\tA description of the La Porte plant and use of substances regulated by EPA's Risk Management Program (RMP) regulation\n*\tAn overview of our accidental release prevention programs\n*\tA five-year accident history for accidental releases of chemicals regulated by EPA's RMP rule\n*\tAn overview of our emergency response program\n*\tAn overview of improvements made at the La Porte plant to minimize the potential for accidental chemical releases that could adversely affect our employees, the public, and the environment\n*\tCertifications in accordance with RMP rule\n*\tRelevant details about the La Porte plant's risk management program\nSTATIONARY SOURCE AND REGULATED SUBSTANCES:\nThe site includes unit operations to produce polypropylene and polyethylene products using multiple chemicals including RMP regulated substances.\n\nOur accidental release prevention programs and our contingency planning efforts help us effectively manage the hazards to employees, the public, and the environment otherwised posed by these chemicals.\n\nGENERAL ACCIDENTAL RELEASE PREVENTION PROGRAM AND CHEMICAL-SPECIFIC PREVENTION STEPS:\n\nThe centerpiece of the accident release and prevention program at the La Porte plant is Process Safety Management. This is a fully comprehensive prevention program designed to prevent accidental releases of hazardous chemicals. We take a systematic, proactive approach to preventing accidental relea"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000119772,
      "SummaryText": "ses of hazardous chemicals. Our management systems address each of the key features of successful prevention programs including:\n*\tProcess safety information\n*\tProcess hazard analysis\n*\tOperating procedures\n*\tTraining\n*\tMechanical integrity\n*\tManagement of change\n*\tPre-startup safety review\n*\tCompliance audits\n*\tIncident investigation\n*\tEmployee participation\n*\tHot work permit\n*\tContractors\nWe actively encourage participation by all employees in process safety management. Relevant process safety information is readily available to employees. Employees participate in PHA revalidation studies and PHA studies consistent with our Management of Change Program.\nProcedures provide knowledge to ensure employees perform their jobs safely and correctly. Employee suggestions for improvement are encouraged. Experienced employees are involved in training. Employees and contractors are trained and are included in the investigation of incidents and accidents.\n\nAs prescribed by our Management of Change procedure, Pre-Startup Safety Reviews ensure that new installations or modifications are safe for startup and continued operation. Our Mechanical Integrity Program includes written procedures to assure that affected process equipment is properly and safely maintained. The La Porte Plant's Hot Work Permit procedure is consistently enforced to prevent hot work in potentially dangerous areas.\n\nThese individual elements of our prevention program work together to minimize accidental chemical\nreleases. Our company and our employees are committed to the standard that these management\nsystems set for the way we do business. We have established specific accountabilities and controls\nin support of our own high standards for accident prevention.\n\nFIVE-YEAR ACCIDENT HISTORY:\nWe keep records for all significant accidental chemical releases that occur at the La Porte plant. In\nthe past five years, there have been no releases of propylene, ethylene, or other RMP regulated\nchemicals at the La Porte"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 2,
      "FacilityID": 1000119772,
      "SummaryText": "Plant that are governed by 40 C.F. R. 68.42(a), or that have had an\nadverse impact on employees, the community, or the environment. If an incident governed by 40\nC.F.R. 68.42(a) were to occur, we would conduct formal incident investigations designed to identify\nand address the root cause.\n\nEMERGENCY RESPONSE PROGRAM:\nWe have developed and maintain an emergency response plan which includes planning and training\nelements for effectively protecting workers, the public, and the environment during emergency\nsituations. Furthermore, we coordinate our plan with the Channel Industries Mutual Aid (CIMA) and\ncommunity emergency response plans (LEPC).\n\nPLANNED CHANGES TO IMPROVE SAFETY\nBraskem believes in a philosophy of continuous improvement. A number of programs are in place to\nmonitor the status of ongoing efforts and continually seek opportunities to improve. The La Porte\nplant maintains employee safety teams that meet regularly and a structured safety process.\nThe Company's current plans to further enhance safety practices during 2025 including Safety campaign focused on hazard Recognition and Human Reliability, improvements and updates to the site\u0092s life critical procedures and associated training curriculum as well as addition of new four-gas meters in support of safe work permitting across the site."
    }
  ]
